st of  these wonder years  serve nutritious wonder enriched bread  white bread  fortified with vitamins to replace the  highly nutritional but easily spoiled  whole grains  quickly became the norm there's two  kinds of agriculture  you can grow food to sell it in which  case you don't really care what it is  because you're just selling it all you  want is  am i making more money selling this than  that and that tells you the answer  or you can grow food to feed yourself or  feed other people  and that's a that's a dividing line our  first  dish is a good example of early  agriculture this is a  sort of mayan inspired or meso-american  inspired stew you know  a form of farming called milpa or three  sisters and the three sisters are corn  squash and beans  the corn grows straight up it provides a  pole for the beans  and the squash provides shade to retain  moisture for everything  and the beans help fix nitrogen in the  soil so it's a it's a  they're a kind of cover crop so those  three things can be grown together  quite sustainably and this  is better than  that i mean this is the perfect  representation of 19th and early 20th  century  processed food you have your white bread  so you have bread that's completely  devoid of nutrients except for whatever  nutrients you put back in  if your industrially produced meat uses  resources disproportionately  encourages the growth of corn and  soybeans and very little else  you have cheese a direct result of the  dairy surplus  and then what do we do with it oh here  we'll do this  a public health disaster represented by  a cheeseburger but  don't get mad at me it it tastes good i  know you love it  i like cheeseburgers too trust me it's  not a problem  we learned to like cheeseburger i grew  up eating cheeseburgers  ever since 1921 people have enjoyed  white castle hamburger easy food like  this has been advertised almost non-stop  for decades  i'm not hungry you're having one more  bite one more bite  efforts to regulate what can or cannot  be marketed specifically to kids have  largely been ineffective  especially now that they do most of  their viewing on streaming devices  no one's really regulating what food  companies market to kids online  before they're really capable of knowing  the difference between right and wrong  that good food fun food comes in  colorful packages  sold by cartoon characters and usually  contains a lot of sugar  until we start teaching four-year-olds  what real food is  how to eat it how to enjoy it what it  takes to prepare it  then we're going to have 40 year olds  who are conflicted about  oh i know what good food is but i grew  up eating  fruit loops and and sometimes you just  want a bowl  all of that brings us to bittman's third  and final stage in food history  change in a way you could see it all as  a great experiment  how is a human race of six  seven ten billion people going to  support itself  and that question in a way has been  answered we can produce enough food  for those people now the question's a  little more refined  how do we produce enough food for those  people and make them healthy  all of them or most of them and how do  we produce enough food  for those people and not destroy the  planet  those two questions have to be answered  in the next  x years which is why today obesity and  its related illnesses is a much bigger  problem for most of the world than  than hunger is which is not to say  hunger is still not a problem  in some places but that's the big  question is how do you get kids  on that track of like eating the best  stuff  real food real food a bowl of loops is  still okay every once in a while we  most people still like cheeseburgers but  generally speaking  how do you how do you balance it out  right well so much of our food  oh it's not good for the planet

